you...@cds.ne.jp wrote:

> "Ever heard hissing outside?  I *knew* you hadn't!"

>  I guess it says someting about a cockatrice, but I cannot
>  understand what the 'outside' means, and why '*knew*' is
>  a past tense and is emphasized.

The question is a _rhetorical_ one, that is,
one to which the questioner already knows
the answer. That knowledge preceded the asking
of the question, thus it is knowledge in the
past, and so the knowing of it is spoken of in
the past tense.

The emphasis is of a teasing sort, creating a
claim that the speaker's knowledge exceeds the
listener's knowledge.

As to being "outside", I'm guessing, but

1) had the listener heard a cockatrice hissing
before entering the dungeon, the listener would
instead be a statue outside the dungeon;

2) perhaps in this mythos, cockatrices only
live in caves? I think in the original mythos,
they are only encountered by moonlight, but I
might be mistaken about that.

